===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|medicine}} A [[patient]] in a state of [[catatonia]].
#* '''1953''', ''Canadian Journal of Psychology: Revue Canadienne de Psychologie'', Volume 7, [http://books.google.com.au/books?id=xlcUAAAAIAAJ&q=%22catatonics%22&dq=%22catatonics%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=0942U7zmMMmmlAWxsIG4AQ&redir_esc=y page 120],
#*: An inspection of Table IV shows that the '''catatonics''' have the lowest mean reversal score of all the groups.
#* '''1973''', [[w:Oliver Sacks|Oliver Sacks]], ''[[w:Awakenings (book)|Awakenings]]'', [http://books.google.com.au/books?id=p1oI7kkY4FkC&pg=PT125&dq=%22I+thought+of+the+Sleeping+Beauty;+and+I+also+thought,+with+some+foreboding,+of+catatonics%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=AuE2U9LVEc7HkQWV84GICw&ved=0CC8Q6AEwAA#v=onepage&q=%22I%20thought%20of%20the%20Sleeping%20Beauty%3B%20and%20I%20also%20thought%2C%20with%20some%20foreboding%2C%20of%20catatonics%22&f=false unnumbered page],
#*: I thought of children released from school; I thought of spring-awakenings after winter-sleeps; I thought of the Sleeping Beauty; and I also thought, with some foreboding, of '''catatonics''', suddenly frenzied.
#* {{quote-text|year=1991|author=Dean Turner|title=Escape from God: The Use of Religion and Philosophy to Evade Responsibility|page=92|url=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=BGU7LZ2bQ4cC&pg=PA92&dq=%22catatonics%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=0942U7zmMMmmlAWxsIG4AQ&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22catatonics%22&f=false|passage=All outward signs suggest that '''catatonics''' have ceased being subjects by virtue of having transformed themselves into veritable objects.}}

